## 1. A fault unwinds a lowered loop's frames in the order they were taken (ruling 4.1)

**What failed.** In Debug, `test/cases/loops/fault.cml` on `std::fvm` and `std::prl` aborted in
`FramePool::release`'s strict-LIFO assertion with

```
Trying to release a frame that is not on top, top frame of graph <main.loop1.next> is at ...
```

The fault is a division by zero inside a round of a `while` loop. The activation that runs the
loop has `main.loop1.body` executing, `main.loop1.next` below it (a frame the loop machinery made
and no return has released), and `main` below that. `callBorrowed`'s catch released
`currFrame` (`body`), then **the activation root** (`main`) — skipping `next` — and only then
popped `frameStack_`. The root was released while a live frame was above it.

**Correction to H31.** H31 4.1 says the `next` frame "is in none of those" (the executing frame,
the root, `frameStack_`), so fixing the order would need a registry of frames a lowering owns.
That premise is wrong: `next` **is** in the activation's `frameStack_`; the faulting call's frame
sits above it, so no return has removed it. The unwinder already has the frame; it released it
in the wrong order. So the fix needs no registry, no lowering change and no frame-owner table:

```
releaseFrame(currFrame);              // body
while (stackDepth_ > stackDepthBase)  // next, then whoever is below it
    releaseFrame(frameStack_[--stackDepth_]);
if (currFrame != rootActiveFrame)     // main, the activation's root, last
    releaseFrame(rootActiveFrame);
```

This is option 1's outcome — the frame is released by the code that created it (the activation
whose open call pushed it) with the pool's strict LIFO kept — reached through the unwinder's own
state rather than through `loop_lowering.cpp`. The coordinator's control-regions work will turn
the loop into a region and delete that file; nothing here depends on it.

**Backends.** The order is fixed in both catches (`RuntimeFault` and `Diagnostic`) of both
interpreters: `gotovm.cpp` (computed goto, the build here) and `casevm.cpp` (the fallback, built
only when `ENABLE_FASTVM_COMPUTED_GOTO` is off; changed so the two do not diverge, but not
compiled in this tree). `std::jit` already passed because `runFramedJit`'s catch released `cur`
and then `frameStack_` top-down; `std::nvm` runs LOOP natively and never had the problem. On the
Debug build all four backends now print the same diagnostic and exit 1:

```
(fault)test\cases\loops\fault.cml:3:61: [Error]: Attempted division by zero. ...
(name=RuntimeDiag::DivisionByZero, code=0x04000001)
```

**Frame evidence (Debug, `-vv --log-include FramePool`).** On `std::fvm` the catch now releases
`main.loop1.body`, then `main.loop1.next`, then `main`; the assertion no longer fires. On
`std::prl` the activation returns to its base: `body`, `next`, `main`, final top equal to the
first acquire's top. Before the change the `main` release was the failing one on both.

**The entry frame.** On the CLI's runtime-fault path the diagnostic sink is configured to stop at
the first error (`runtimeDiagsConfig`, error limit 0), so what leaves the outermost activation is
the sink's limit exception, not a `Diagnostic`. The unwinder does not catch it, and the entry
`__root__` frame of the CLI's activation is therefore not released before the process exits. That
is not a frame a loop created, it happens for any top-level fault (a plain `1 / 0` in `main`
shows it too), and it was true before this change; the process is ending. The `Diagnostic` path
(fault isolation / described faults) has the same corrected order and does release the root.

## 2. `Frame::get` checks for `DeadSlot` too (H31 7.1)

`Frame::get` asserted only `dynamicArea_[idx] != mm::kDebugUninitializedSlot`. A fresh Debug bump
allocation is filled with `DeadSlot` (`0xDEADBEAFDEADBEAF`), while the Debug taskflow frame pool
fills a frame with `kDebugUninitializedSlot` (`0xDEADBEEFDEADBEEF`) before use; only
`rtdata::fromSlot` compared `DeadSlot`, so a read of a slot written by neither pattern was caught
one call later than the frame check. Both `Frame::get` and `FrameView::get` now test both fills,
with a comment naming them and stating that `NullSlot` is the legitimate empty element (unchanged
semantics: it still reads as an empty slot). No new abort site is reachable by a correct program —
`fromSlot` already refused `DeadSlot` — so the change moves the frame-side check to the same
invariant, not the definition of a legitimate empty. The Debug tier's 324 passes include the read
paths that see genuinely empty slots and views.

## 3. The frame log stops printing whole arrays (H31 7.2)

**The cap is 64 elements**, `kFrameLogArrayLimit` in `frame.h`. Above it an array prints as

```
<int[] at 0x20a1c7452c0, 10000 elements>
```

at every depth of the value (arrays nested in tuples, structs or arrays are bounded too). At or
below the cap the elements still print: `reduce_view.cml` logs `[(1, 30), (2, 6), (3, 20)]`.

**Why 64.** The largest array any plan expects printed whole is 40 elements (the squares 0..39 in
`feat/para/core.plan.toml`); diagnostic-sized arrays in the plans are smaller. The collections
whose element-by-element logging made a Debug run quadratic are thousands: H31 names 10,000
(`hof_compact_element_view`) and 5,000 (`inside_recursion`). 64 keeps every diagnostic-sized
array whole with margin and bounds one log line to 64 elements.

**How.** `rtdata::printSlotCapped` shares the printer body with `printValue`
(`printValueImpl(..., arrayLimit)`); `printValue` delegates with `size_t` maximum, so `println`,
`format` and `string(x)` are unchanged and still print whole arrays. The new function is exported
in `cmake/libcamel_exports.def` (its mangled name was computed from the signature and the Release
link accepted it). Only the frame log's GET line uses the capped entry.

**Evidence.** `hof_block_alloc.cml` (10,000 elements) with `-vv --log-include Frame` on Debug:
400,062 log lines, 38.7 MB, 9.7 s; every line that mentions the big arrays is the one-line form
above instead of carrying 10,000 elements. The growth is now linear in the number of reads
instead of quadratic in the array size (H31 measured 240 s for the same class of run before its
eager-text fix; this is the bound that remains when the log is actually enabled). No plan asserts
the frame log's text, and the print-format plans pass unchanged.

